# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Ecclesiastes'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'poetry'
SUBTITLE = 'Life Under the Sun, the Gift of God, and the Whole Duty of Man'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Ecclesiastes is “The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem” (Eccl. 1:1). He declares that “all is vanity” (Eccl. 1:2), weighs what is done under the sun, gives a time to every purpose, counsels reverence and gladness in youth, and closes with the conclusion of the whole matter (Eccl. 3:1; 11:9; 12:13–14).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'The book is introduced as the words of the Preacher, the son of David, king over Israel in Jerusalem (Eccl. 1:1, 12). Its reflections on life under the sun are placed in the Universal Truths Table; the verses that name the speaker’s setting are program-labeled.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Ecclesiastes', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Vanity Under the Sun (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'The Preacher sets out his search: “Vanity of vanities… all is vanity” (Eccl. 1:2, 9; 2:24).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — A Time for Everything (Chs. 3–5)'), ('p', '“To every thing there is a season,” and God has made everything beautiful in His time; the Preacher counsels that words before God be few (Eccl. 3:1, 11, 14; 5:2).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The House of Mourning (Chs. 6–8)'), ('p', 'It is better to go to the house of mourning, and God made man upright (Eccl. 7:2, 29).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Do It with Your Might (Chs. 9–11)'), ('p', 'Whatever the hand finds to do is to be done with might, and the young are told that God will bring them into judgment (Eccl. 9:10; 11:9).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Remember Thy Creator (Ch. 12)'), ('p', '“Remember now thy Creator in the days of thy youth,” and the conclusion of the whole matter is given (Eccl. 12:1, 7, 13–14).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Ecclesiastes', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Vanity</b> — “Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity” (Eccl. 1:2).', '<b>Nothing New Under the Sun</b> — “There is no new thing under the sun” (Eccl. 1:9).', '<b>A Season for Every Purpose</b> — “To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven” (Eccl. 3:1).', '<b>God’s Works Endure</b> — “Whatsoever God doeth, it shall be for ever” (Eccl. 3:14).', '<b>Reverence in Words</b> — “Be not rash with thy mouth… let thy words be few” (Eccl. 5:2).', '<b>Judgment Coming</b> — “God will bring thee into judgment” (Eccl. 11:9).', '<b>The Whole Duty</b> — “Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man” (Eccl. 12:13).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Ecclesiastes', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Ecclesiastes points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Son of David</b> — The Preacher is named “the son of David, king in Jerusalem” (Eccl. 1:1); the book is reported as written and no further identification is made.', '<b>The Spirit Returns to God</b> — “The spirit shall return unto God who gave it” (Eccl. 12:7).', '<b>Judgment of Every Work</b> — “God shall bring every work into judgment” (Eccl. 12:14).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Ecclesiastes', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Ecclesiastes speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Ecclesiastes operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Ecclesiastes in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Ecclesiastes reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the speaker as son of David and king (Eccl. 1:1)', 'the speaker as king over Israel in Jerusalem (Eccl. 1:12)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Ecclesiastes that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Ecclesiastes contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Preacher — “The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.” (Eccl. 1:1).', 'King over Israel — “I the Preacher was king over Israel in Jerusalem.” (Eccl. 1:12).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Ecclesiastes Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Ecclesiastes and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Ecclesiastes teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Ecclesiastes', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Preacher', '“The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Eccl. 1:1'], ['King over Israel', '“I the Preacher was king over Israel in Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Eccl. 1:12']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Ecclesiastes that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Ecclesiastes teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Vanity of Vanities', '“Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity.”', 'Eccl. 1:2'], ['A Time for Everything', '“To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven.”', 'Eccl. 3:1'], ['Eternity in the Heart', '“He hath made every thing beautiful in his time.”', 'Eccl. 3:11'], ['God’s Work Endures', '“whatsoever God doeth, it shall be for ever.”', 'Eccl. 3:14'], ['Reverent Words', '“let thy words be few.”', 'Eccl. 5:2'], ['The House of Mourning', '“It is better to go to the house of mourning, than to go to the house of feasting.”', 'Eccl. 7:2'], ['Man Made Upright', '“God hath made man upright; but they have sought out many inventions.”', 'Eccl. 7:29'], ['Do It with Might', '“Whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might.”', 'Eccl. 9:10'], ['Remember the Creator', '“Remember now thy Creator in the days of thy youth.”', 'Eccl. 12:1'], ['The Spirit Returns', '“the spirit shall return unto God who gave it.”', 'Eccl. 12:7'], ['The Whole Duty of Man', '“Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man.”', 'Eccl. 12:13'], ['Every Work Judged', '“God shall bring every work into judgment.”', 'Eccl. 12:14']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Ecclesiastes without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Ecclesiastes in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Receive life as a gift of God</b> — Eat, drink and enjoy good in labour (Eccl. 2:24).', '<b>Keep reverent words</b> — Let thy words be few (Eccl. 5:2).', '<b>Learn from mourning</b> — The living lay it to heart (Eccl. 7:2).', '<b>Work with all your might</b> — Whatsoever thy hand findeth (Eccl. 9:10).', '<b>Remember the Creator early</b> — In the days of thy youth (Eccl. 12:1).', '<b>Fear God</b> — The whole duty of man (Eccl. 12:13).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Ecclesiastes', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Ecclesiastes in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Vanity Under the Sun (Chs. 1–2) — The Preacher’s search. <i>(Eccl. 1:1–2, 9; 2:24)</i>', 'A Time for Everything (Chs. 3–5) — God’s seasons and reverent words. <i>(Eccl. 3:1, 11, 14; 5:2)</i>', 'The House of Mourning (Chs. 6–8) — Sober counsel. <i>(Eccl. 7:2, 29)</i>', 'Do It with Might (Chs. 9–11) — Work, joy and judgment. <i>(Eccl. 9:10; 11:9)</i>', 'Remember Thy Creator (Ch. 12) — The whole duty of man. <i>(Eccl. 12:1, 7, 13–14)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Ecclesiastes, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward sobriety, gratitude, and the fear of God without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Ecclesiastes simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Ecclesiastes stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
